==English==

===Etymology===
From a monologue delivered by the character [[w:Polonius|Polonius]] in Act I Scene III of ''[[w:Hamlet|Hamlet]]'' by [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]].

===Proverb===
{{head|en|proverb}}

# [[be oneself|Be yourself]]; be [[true]] to yourself; do not engage in [[self-deception]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|I|III|text=This above all: '''to thine ownself be true''',<br>And it must follow, as the night the day,<br>Thou canst not then be false to any man.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1977|editor=Robert P Marinelli; Arthur E. Dell Orto|title=The Psychological and Social Impact of Physical Disability|page=306|publisher=Springer Publishing Co.|isbn=9780826122100
|passage="'''To thine own self be true'''," I saw, was what produced vitality, confidence, and genuine expression in one's interpersonal relations.}}
#* '''1986''', Gary Diedrichs, "[http://books.google.com/books?id=jxIEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA27 Bewitched]", ''Orange Coast'', August 1986:
#*: Know thyself. '''To thine own self be true'''. For the man or woman who can confront the demon within, there is a hopeful prognosis.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=[[w:Paula Rodriguez Rust|Paula C. Rust]]|title=Bisexuality and the Challenge to Lesbian Politics: Sex, Loyalty, and Revolution|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=U7qt-VKvDc8C&pg=PA51|page=51|publisher=New York University Press|year_published=1995|isbn=081477444X
|passage=Several of these women said simply, "to each her own," while others like Sue were only slighty more verbose: "Each of us has a right and a responsibility ''''to thine own self be true'''.' Another person's sexual preference is not my business or concern."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=James M. Morris|author2=Andrea L. Kross|title=Historical Dictionary of Utopianism|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=odgOl06HasQC&pg=PA262|page=262|publisher=Scarecrow Press|year_published=2004|isbn=0810849127
|passage="'''To thine own self be true'''" whatever the consequences was taken as the principle of true freedom and humanity by the romantics.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|editor=Jonathan J. Sanford|author=Mark D. White|chapter=The Sound and the Fury Behind 'One More Day'|title=Spider-Man and Philosophy: The Web of Inquiry|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=oEHbCwcV-NEC&pg=PA241|page=241|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|isbn=9780470575604
|passage=As Shakespeare wrote, "'''To thine own self be true'''," at least according to what kind of person you believe yourself to be.}}

